  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nIR currently shows ample cloud coverage pushing in from the east\nspilling over the upper level ridge to the south as it\nprogresses further east. The main PVA anomalies of the main \ncore of the system will be more constrained to north and \nnortheastern Utah but will skirt to the south on Thursday. The \nmain finger of the PFJ support the upper level low over the \nPacific northwest with a 130kt jet max pushing into its base \nacross central California allowing for it to continue to deepen \nwith increasing divergence aloft. This trend looks to continue \nThursday with its slow eastward progression but becomes more \npositively tilted and open into the overnight hours into Friday\nmorning. Being more progressive, any extensive impacts look to \nbe limited for Friday as advisory level at best snowfall for \nwestern mountains and passes the likely hazards to be had. This \nwill be looked at once again in the next cycle or two but \nholding off at this time with the lower confidence in impacts \nwith a longer duration and lower snowfall rates. \n\nAs the system nears into early Thursday morning, expect the\nlight snow to push in from Idaho across western mountains with\nlimited chances for lower valleys throughout the day. Seasonable\ntemperatures and cloudy skies throughout expected regardless as\nwinds continue to the east for the wind corridor. Gusts over 30\nmph will be common from eastern Sweetwater near South Pass and\nacross eastern Fremont to Natrona Counties. Not near the winds\nas Wednesday but gusty conditions for the afternoon and into the\nevening hours will be of high confidence with the approaching\nsystem. Overnight into Friday, expect the aforementioned to\nbecome more open and lose some of its juice as it digs further\nsouth and a cold front pushes through during daylight hours.\nWrap around east flow will allow for minor chances for snowfall\neast of the Divide Friday, mainly for the higher elevations.\n30-40% probabilities for over 6 inches across the Bighorns, but\nlower to around 2 to 4 inches for the passes. Much of the impact\nwill be for western mountains of higher confidence over 4 to 6\ninches for Teton and Togwotee Passes around 70-80%. This is\nwhere advisories will be likely for Friday morning and much of\nthe rest of the day as snow lingers through the overnight hours\nand into the weekend. Breezy winds for the wind corridor\ncontinue as well Friday and into the weekend with other areas\nremaining relatively light in wake of the frontal boundary.\n\nThe weekend looks to relatively dry as the system exits to the \neast with lingering snow ending early Saturday morning. Induced \nupper level ridging expected ahead of the next system for early \nnext week. Temperatures remain just below to near average over \nthe weekend as well and into early next week. The next system \nlooks to impact areas to the west by early Monday and extending \neast of the Divide later in the day. Details unknown with timing\nand accumulations, but expect another widespread chance for \nprecipitation to include both side of the Divide with the \nsouthward trend of these systems allowing for the wrap around \nearly flow to the east. The PFJ remains across the area \nextending out of the Pacific northwest with a continued active \npattern for the long term forecast. More induced ridging \nTuesday with more chances mid to end of next work week as well \nwith the next low comes out of the Gulf of Alaska into the \nnorthwest to continue that trend.",
